course: dictionary
lesson: 14531
title: Keeping things going
level: primary-6
stars: 0
explain: To perpetuate something means to make it continue for a long time. It is a formal word, so you will meet it more in school discussions, history and writing than in chat. «Repeating the rumour that the bridge is unsafe only perpetuates a myth.» The rumour keeps going because people keep repeating it.
explain: Perpetuate often goes with things that are bad or untrue, such as a myth, an unfair idea or a problem. «Unfair jokes about boys who cry can perpetuate a harmful stereotype.» Each time the joke is repeated, the stereotype stays alive. When you read perpetuate, ask: what is staying alive or going on for a long time?
explain: Perpetuate can also mean keeping a memory or a name alive. «The statue in the square perpetuates the memory of the brave firefighters.» The statue is there so people will remember them for years. Here the word is not about something bad, but about keeping something good remembered.
fact: To perpetuate means to make something continue for a long time.
fact: Perpetuate is often used with bad things, like a myth, an unfair idea or a problem.
fact: To perpetuate a memory or a name means to keep it alive, as in «The statue perpetuates his memory.»
fact: «Perpetuate a stereotype» means keep an unfair idea going by repeating it.
fact: Perpetuate is a formal word, so it is most common in school discussions, history and writing.
exercise: «Repeating the rumour that the bridge is unsafe only perpetuates a myth.» What does perpetuates mean here? | Proves the story is true | Ends a story quickly | Writes a new book | *Keeps a false story going for a long time
exercise: «The statue in the square perpetuates the memory of the brave firefighters.» What does the statue help to do? | Melt in the rain | *Keep the firefighters' memory alive | Build a new fire station | Make the firefighters forget their work
exercise: «Unfair jokes can perpetuate a harmful stereotype.» Which phrase best explains this? | Write a book about stereotypes | Make a joke about a stereotype | *Keep an unfair idea going by repeating it | Stop an unfair idea straight away
test: Which word means to make something continue for a long time? | Pause | Shorten | *Perpetuate | Vanish
test: «The plaque on the wall perpetuates the name of the school's first head teacher.» What does the plaque do? | Shows a map of the school | *Keeps the name alive for many years | Asks people to change the name | Removes the name from the school
exam: «Repeating an unfair idea about girls and sport can perpetuate a stereotype, so think before you say it.» What does perpetuate mean here? | Stop suddenly | *Keep going for a long time | Argue about | Forget
